WEBVTT

1
00:00:08.737 --> 00:00:10.197
Thank you.

2
00:00:33.197 --> 00:00:34.897
Thank you.

3
00:02:09.417 --> 00:02:11.037
My Chris.

4
00:02:11.437 --> 00:02:13.497
Oh, hello, Janet.

5
00:02:16.797 --> 00:02:17.997
Explain yourself?

6
00:02:18.077 --> 00:02:20.197
Explain what? Rooting on the balcony.

7
00:02:20.277 --> 00:02:21.957
Just isn't being done this season.

8
00:02:22.037 --> 00:02:23.197
Oh, isn't doing?

9
00:02:23.397 --> 00:02:25.997
Lying to one's sisters after season two.

10
00:02:26.757 --> 00:02:28.237
Strangest thing happened.

11
00:02:28.317 --> 00:02:32.937
I thought I heard a man's voice
out over the ocean, calling my name.

12
00:02:33.017 --> 00:02:35.617
Next time he calls, ask him
if he has a friend for me.

13
00:02:35.697 --> 00:02:36.917
I'm serious.

14
00:02:36.997 --> 00:02:38.617
How can you be serious?

15
00:02:38.777 --> 00:02:41.217
It was the wind. Are those bobb horns?

16
00:02:41.297 --> 00:02:42.317
Perhaps.

17
00:02:42.657 --> 00:02:44.357
But it sounded like...

18
00:02:45.577 --> 00:02:46.597
Paul.

19
00:02:49.377 --> 00:02:51.777
It's been two years now, Christine.

20
00:02:51.857 --> 00:02:52.997
I know.

21
00:02:53.937 --> 00:02:59.797
But for a moment, out over the ocean,
I felt very close to him again.

22
00:03:01.137 --> 00:03:04.490
He used to love the ocean,
you know, especially at night.

23
00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:08.793
He'd go racing down to the beach for
a swim, no matter how late it was.

24
00:03:09.237 --> 00:03:11.943
And usually I'd be fool
enough to go with him.

25
00:03:13.137 --> 00:03:16.597
If any husband of mine ever chased me
into the ocean in the middle of the night,

26
00:03:16.677 --> 00:03:18.017
I'd shoot him.

27
00:03:18.597 --> 00:03:22.417
He did a lot of crazy
things, some of them bad.

28
00:03:23.417 --> 00:03:27.377
But I don't think I could ever love
another man the way I love Paul.

29
00:03:27.457 --> 00:03:30.277
Chris, you've got to
stop talking like this.

30
00:03:30.557 --> 00:03:32.037
You're getting morbid.

31
00:03:32.117 --> 00:03:35.057
Oh, it's not as bad as all rat-rat.

32
00:03:35.497 --> 00:03:37.177
It is bad.

33
00:03:37.917 --> 00:03:39.677
Look at this dress.

34
00:03:39.937 --> 00:03:42.457
You can't wear black when
you're going out on a date.

35
00:03:42.537 --> 00:03:44.497
Looks as if you were still...

36
00:03:44.577 --> 00:03:45.857
well...

37
00:03:45.937 --> 00:03:47.197
In the morning?

38
00:03:47.277 --> 00:03:48.337
Well, yes.

39
00:03:48.497 --> 00:03:51.077
I just happened to like black, that's all.

40
00:03:52.497 --> 00:03:55.877
But just for tonight,
it isn't exactly appropriate.

41
00:03:56.417 --> 00:04:00.737
And Chris, don't wear your
wedding ring tonight either.

42
00:04:00.817 --> 00:04:02.017
Why not?

43
00:04:02.517 --> 00:04:04.817
It might make it awkward for Martin.

44
00:04:05.397 --> 00:04:07.937
Is Martin going to propose again tonight?

45
00:04:08.017 --> 00:04:09.297
Well, he might.

46
00:04:10.297 --> 00:04:13.677
And he can't very well put an engagement
ring on your finger if that's already

47
00:04:13.757 --> 00:04:17.757
there. By the way, what kind of
a ring has Martin bought for me?

48
00:04:17.877 --> 00:04:19.057
Enormous diamond with...

49
00:04:19.137 --> 00:04:22.437
Oh, Chris, you will say
yes tonight, won't you?

50
00:04:22.517 --> 00:04:24.037
I suppose I'd better.

51
00:04:24.117 --> 00:04:26.737
Otherwise, he might sue
you for breach of promise.

52
00:04:26.817 --> 00:04:28.397
Oh, darling, I'm so excited.

53
00:04:28.477 --> 00:04:31.830
And I've got just the right
dress all picked out for you.

54
00:04:32.437 --> 00:04:33.577
Were you worried?

55
00:04:33.657 --> 00:04:36.951
Anything you say. And bring
out my new ermine, will you?

56
00:04:40.337 --> 00:04:44.057
You know, Chris, I think your cloak
with the sequins might look better on you

57
00:04:44.137 --> 00:04:45.137
tonight.

58
00:04:45.537 --> 00:04:48.697
I, uh, take it you have
a date for tonight, too.

59
00:04:49.137 --> 00:04:50.837
In fact, I have. All right.

60
00:04:50.917 --> 00:04:53.117
I'll wear the secrets.
You wear the urbans.

61
00:04:53.197 --> 00:04:54.737
Well, if you insist.

62
00:04:55.117 --> 00:04:56.117
Thanks.

63
00:04:56.357 --> 00:04:58.397
I like your hair like that.

64
00:04:58.597 --> 00:05:00.637
But you could do with
a little more lipstick.

65
00:05:00.717 --> 00:05:03.237
And, uh, you could do with a little less.

66
00:05:08.777 --> 00:05:10.257
Do you suppose it's Martin?

67
00:05:10.337 --> 00:05:12.697
You know, Martin would
never honk a horn at a lady.

68
00:05:12.777 --> 00:05:14.483
It's probably your young man.

69
00:05:14.617 --> 00:05:16.977
By the way, which young man is it tonight?

70
00:05:17.057 --> 00:05:19.057
It's... You know, I've forgotten.

71
00:05:19.137 --> 00:05:20.897
Have a good time and come home early.

72
00:05:20.977 --> 00:05:23.697
Well, I can't do both.
Then you come home early.

73
00:05:23.777 --> 00:05:25.677
You come home engaged.

74
00:05:39.397 --> 00:05:40.917
Mrs. Faber's residence.

75
00:05:42.657 --> 00:05:43.897
Yes, sir.

76
00:05:46.137 --> 00:05:47.657
Mrs. Faber.

77
00:05:52.417 --> 00:05:53.397
It's Mr.

78
00:05:53.477 --> 00:05:54.777
Rabbitman. Thank you, Emily.

79
00:05:54.857 --> 00:05:55.917
I'll take it here.

80
00:05:57.617 --> 00:06:00.357
Hello, Martin. Don't tell
me you're standing me up.

81
00:06:00.437 --> 00:06:04.737
No, no, it's just that some idiotic client
came into the office at the last minute

82
00:06:04.817 --> 00:06:05.837
and I...

83
00:06:06.217 --> 00:06:11.317
Well, no, I know it's not serious, but I
particularly didn't want to have anything

84
00:06:11.397 --> 00:06:12.777
interfere with tonight.

85
00:06:12.977 --> 00:06:16.297
Well, no, you see, I wanted to show you
that I'm not always the lawyer with his mind

86
00:06:16.377 --> 00:06:18.997
on cases and affidavits.

87
00:06:19.177 --> 00:06:22.557
No, I'm really the romantic
type with my mind on you.

88
00:06:22.917 --> 00:06:23.917
Yeah,

89
00:06:24.517 --> 00:06:26.137
I thought it was a nice beach.

90
00:06:26.217 --> 00:06:28.737
I dreamed it up this
afternoon in felony court.

91
00:06:28.817 --> 00:06:30.657
It doesn't matter if we're
a little late, Martin.

92
00:06:30.737 --> 00:06:32.561
The first act's no good anyway.

93
00:06:32.877 --> 00:06:35.837
I'll walk over along the beach to your house,
then you won't have to waste the time

94
00:06:35.917 --> 00:06:37.217
driving over here.

95
00:06:37.297 --> 00:06:39.837
Oh, I wouldn't do that.

96
00:06:39.917 --> 00:06:41.437
But I want to walk over, Martin.

97
00:06:41.517 --> 00:06:42.777
I'll enjoy it.

98
00:06:43.657 --> 00:06:45.177
Of course it's safe.

99
00:06:45.257 --> 00:06:47.137
Now stop talking like an old maid.

100
00:06:47.217 --> 00:06:48.217
Goodbye.

101
00:06:49.137 --> 00:06:50.737
There's dark down there, ma'am.

102
00:06:50.817 --> 00:06:53.517
And you, Emily, stop
talking like Martin Abbott.

103
00:06:53.597 --> 00:06:55.357
Lock the patio door after me, will you?

104
00:06:55.437 --> 00:06:56.437
Good night.

105
00:07:34.957 --> 00:07:38.297
Oh, my God.

106
00:09:16.817 --> 00:09:18.717
Chris!

107
00:09:19.677 --> 00:09:21.577
Chris!

108
00:09:47.537 --> 00:09:49.831
I don't blame you for being frightened.

109
00:09:49.917 --> 00:09:52.537
You made a decision
tonight that angered him.

110
00:09:52.617 --> 00:09:54.997
And now you're fighting to sustain it.

111
00:09:55.097 --> 00:09:58.557
Well, you have all my hopes
fighting with you, madame.

112
00:10:02.377 --> 00:10:03.997
I felt you were married.

113
00:10:04.077 --> 00:10:08.717
I saw your husband dead and you
moving toward a new marriage.

114
00:10:09.317 --> 00:10:11.197
And I see I was wrong.

115
00:10:12.657 --> 00:10:13.657
Yeah.

116
00:10:13.877 --> 00:10:14.897
Good night.

117
00:10:14.977 --> 00:10:16.297
And goodbye.

118
00:10:21.817 --> 00:10:22.877
Wait.

119
00:10:23.877 --> 00:10:25.377
You weren't mistaken.

120
00:10:25.457 --> 00:10:27.557
My husband died two years ago.

121
00:10:27.637 --> 00:10:29.597
I felt I could not be wrong.

122
00:10:29.677 --> 00:10:34.097
I saw him dead and his car in flames.

123
00:10:36.477 --> 00:10:39.577
I'm sorry. I don't understand
how you know all this.

124
00:10:39.657 --> 00:10:41.957
I suppose people do talk.

125
00:10:43.237 --> 00:10:45.517
No, I wish I did live on the ocean.

126
00:10:46.197 --> 00:10:48.337
Here is my name and where I live.

127
00:10:52.137 --> 00:10:54.157
Alexis. Psychic consultant.

128
00:10:55.257 --> 00:10:56.257
Oh.

129
00:10:56.357 --> 00:11:00.077
I see you place me in the same
category with fortune tellers,

130
00:11:00.157 --> 00:11:02.037
snake charmers and magicians.

131
00:11:02.317 --> 00:11:04.457
Oh, well, many people do.

132
00:11:04.537 --> 00:11:06.697
But you must know who I am.

133
00:11:06.777 --> 00:11:09.037
How else would you know all these things?

134
00:11:09.117 --> 00:11:11.411
Perhaps because we are very much alike.

135
00:11:13.537 --> 00:11:15.437
Free spirits, both of us.

136
00:11:15.517 --> 00:11:17.337
Like my friend here.

137
00:11:17.777 --> 00:11:21.077
We like the night and
the mist of the ocean.

138
00:11:21.317 --> 00:11:26.297
The wind whispers to us and the
sand feels cool under our feet.

139
00:11:26.837 --> 00:11:31.677
We're not like, I don't know,
I have his name now, like Martin.

140
00:11:31.757 --> 00:11:33.517
There's nothing wrong with Martin.

141
00:11:33.597 --> 00:11:34.977
Of course not.

142
00:11:35.057 --> 00:11:38.957
If only he would understand
how little he understands.

143
00:11:39.057 --> 00:11:41.157
Well, Martin is very logical.

144
00:11:42.657 --> 00:11:44.539
That's why you should marry him.

145
00:11:45.837 --> 00:11:50.337
Even free spirits have to come in out of
the night sometimes and put on their shoes,

146
00:11:50.417 --> 00:11:55.237
pay their bills, go to the dentist,
and, of course, family dinners on Sunday.

147
00:11:55.717 --> 00:11:59.417
You really shouldn't be so
irritated by his little mannerisms,

148
00:11:59.797 --> 00:12:02.117
like when he clears his throat,

149
00:12:03.017 --> 00:12:06.617
announcing that he's going to kiss you
any minute, or when he counts off all his

150
00:12:06.697 --> 00:12:08.817
plans and the tips of his fingers.

151
00:12:10.037 --> 00:12:13.277
How? Well, I can't tell
you how I know these things.

152
00:12:14.177 --> 00:12:16.237
But it hardly matters, does it,

153
00:12:16.517 --> 00:12:18.157
since we're not going to meet again.

154
00:12:18.237 --> 00:12:19.237
Christine?

155
00:12:21.997 --> 00:12:22.997
Chris?

156
00:12:23.577 --> 00:12:25.457
Oh, over here, Martin.

157
00:12:26.877 --> 00:12:28.237
Oh, hi.

158
00:12:28.317 --> 00:12:29.317
Hello.

159
00:12:34.857 --> 00:12:36.337
You certainly had me scared.

160
00:12:36.417 --> 00:12:39.652
What kept you so long? Well,
I was talking to this man.

161
00:12:39.857 --> 00:12:41.477
Well, he was here.

162
00:12:42.317 --> 00:12:43.977
Could I have imagined him?

163
00:12:44.057 --> 00:12:46.177
He was fantastic enough.

164
00:12:47.517 --> 00:12:49.357
Well, let's get going.

165
00:12:49.437 --> 00:12:51.597
Oh, I, I've torn my dress, Martin.

166
00:12:51.677 --> 00:12:53.137
I'll have to go back to the house.

167
00:12:53.217 --> 00:12:54.217
All right.

168
00:12:54.297 --> 00:12:56.117
We'll go to your house first.

169
00:12:56.197 --> 00:12:59.117
Skip the theater,
directly to the Blue Angel.

170
00:13:01.377 --> 00:13:02.537
What's the matter?

171
00:13:02.617 --> 00:13:03.617
Nothing.

172
00:13:03.737 --> 00:13:06.796
It's just that it's so late,
and I'm a little upset.

173
00:13:08.377 --> 00:13:09.557
Of course.

174
00:13:10.297 --> 00:13:11.657
You sure you understand?

175
00:13:11.737 --> 00:13:15.737
When it concerns you, Christine,
there's nothing I don't understand.

176
00:13:24.757 --> 00:13:28.057
Here, you better take this off my hands
or I'll have to wear it to the office

177
00:13:28.137 --> 00:13:29.417
tomorrow. Fix yourself a drink.

178
00:13:29.497 --> 00:13:30.817
I've got to change this torn dress.

179
00:13:30.897 --> 00:13:31.897
Right.

180
00:13:58.297 --> 00:13:59.517
Come on.

181
00:14:00.957 --> 00:14:02.377
Martin, how flattering.

182
00:14:02.457 --> 00:14:04.157
I should have thought of
this in the first place.

183
00:14:04.237 --> 00:14:06.531
This is much better than any nightclub.

184
00:14:07.157 --> 00:14:10.037
No head waiter, no smoke, no people.

185
00:14:10.117 --> 00:14:12.437
And at the end of the evening, no check.

186
00:14:12.517 --> 00:14:15.457
Oh, Martin, you think
in such practical terms.

187
00:14:15.537 --> 00:14:17.397
I'm a very practical man.

188
00:14:17.477 --> 00:14:20.301
For instance, I just said
that to make you pout.

189
00:14:21.037 --> 00:14:22.817
The better to do this.

190
00:14:26.357 --> 00:14:28.217
I've been trapped. Right.

191
00:14:28.357 --> 00:14:31.377
Now, if you'll just step over
here to the sofa by the fire.

192
00:14:31.457 --> 00:14:33.297
Another trap? Definitely.

193
00:15:05.157 --> 00:15:06.137
Darling, what's the matter?

194
00:15:06.217 --> 00:15:07.257
That music.

195
00:15:07.377 --> 00:15:09.797
It's just the phonograph.
But we don't have that record.

196
00:15:09.877 --> 00:15:12.995
Of course you do. It was
right on top of the cabinet.

197
00:15:13.977 --> 00:15:14.917
That's strange.

198
00:15:14.997 --> 00:15:16.497
What's the matter, Chris?

199
00:15:17.417 --> 00:15:21.177
I'm sorry, but that was
Paul's favorite prelude.

200
00:15:21.257 --> 00:15:24.963
I've heard him play it a hundred
times right here in this room.

201
00:15:25.077 --> 00:15:26.537
Turn it off, will you?

202
00:15:28.177 --> 00:15:29.617
No, I won't.

203
00:15:32.897 --> 00:15:34.037
Look, darling.

204
00:15:34.657 --> 00:15:37.657
Paul's been gone too long
for you to act like this.

205
00:15:38.057 --> 00:15:39.357
I know.

206
00:15:41.337 --> 00:15:46.517
Chris, I'm not saying this for my sake,
but your whole life's ahead of you.

207
00:15:46.597 --> 00:15:49.457
You can't waste it probing into the past.

208
00:15:50.237 --> 00:15:52.617
If I can't make you forget Paul,

209
00:15:53.057 --> 00:15:55.417
well, find someone else who can.

210
00:15:56.237 --> 00:15:57.797
You're right, Martin.

211
00:15:57.877 --> 00:16:00.257
I'm sorry I acted like a child.

212
00:16:01.197 --> 00:16:04.737
Would you get me a drink?
Right here, ready for you.

213
00:16:09.157 --> 00:16:12.437
So you think I should
go find somebody new?

214
00:16:15.117 --> 00:16:17.297
Uh, only if it's absolutely necessary.

215
00:16:17.377 --> 00:16:22.117
But I've been so out of touch
with people lately, especially men.

216
00:16:22.197 --> 00:16:24.377
What do you expect me
to do, bring some around?

217
00:16:24.457 --> 00:16:26.637
Well, that would be very nice of you.

218
00:16:26.717 --> 00:16:29.952
Only you know how hard it
is for me to meet new people.

219
00:16:30.477 --> 00:16:31.477
Uh,

220
00:16:32.797 --> 00:16:33.797
well,

221
00:16:33.997 --> 00:16:35.937
I can see I'll just
have to propose to you.

222
00:16:36.017 --> 00:16:37.817
Oh, Martin, I talked you into it.

223
00:16:37.897 --> 00:16:42.597
You sure did. And very fortunately for
you, I just happened to have an engagement

224
00:16:42.677 --> 00:16:43.877
ring on me.

225
00:16:52.997 --> 00:16:54.737
Martin, it's lovely.

226
00:17:04.257 --> 00:17:05.537
Thank you.

227
00:17:40.777 --> 00:17:42.777
Where is this?

228
00:17:53.797 --> 00:17:55.617
Yes, Paul.

229
00:17:56.657 --> 00:17:59.177
Can't you hear the ocean calling?

230
00:17:59.897 --> 00:18:01.897
Come on, Chris.

231
00:18:02.017 --> 00:18:04.057
Race you to the beach.

232
00:18:04.517 --> 00:18:06.997
That's when it's a coward.

233
00:20:29.017 --> 00:20:30.917
Help!

234
00:20:33.077 --> 00:20:34.477
Help!

235
00:20:55.517 --> 00:20:57.497
Chris, darling.

236
00:20:58.997 --> 00:21:01.097
Janet. What happened?

237
00:21:01.177 --> 00:21:02.717
I don't know.

238
00:21:05.557 --> 00:21:07.077
The music.

239
00:21:07.317 --> 00:21:08.337
Paul.

240
00:21:08.417 --> 00:21:10.177
Let me help you up.

241
00:21:12.517 --> 00:21:15.597
Look, darling, you must
have had a nightmare.

242
00:21:15.677 --> 00:21:20.297
It's all right. It's over,
you're awake, and I'm here.

243
00:21:20.377 --> 00:21:22.377
Janet, it wasn't a dream.

244
00:21:22.637 --> 00:21:24.137
The windows blew open.

245
00:21:24.217 --> 00:21:26.857
What's so unusual about
a window blowing open?

246
00:21:26.937 --> 00:21:29.077
My engagement ring was gone.

247
00:21:29.237 --> 00:21:31.157
The flowers were all with it.

248
00:21:31.637 --> 00:21:33.377
Chris, look.

249
00:21:37.137 --> 00:21:39.137
But the picture,

250
00:21:39.637 --> 00:21:41.117
Paul's picture.

251
00:21:45.477 --> 00:21:47.977
You see, just a bad dream.

252
00:21:48.197 --> 00:21:49.917
I was awake.

253
00:21:50.517 --> 00:21:53.837
My wedding gown there in the
closet, it came toward me.

254
00:21:53.917 --> 00:21:55.597
I swear it did.

255
00:21:59.017 --> 00:22:01.037
It's just your bathrobe.

256
00:22:01.177 --> 00:22:06.197
You probably pulled the door open
like this, and it flung out like that.

257
00:22:07.637 --> 00:22:09.757
My wedding dress isn't there?

258
00:22:09.837 --> 00:22:11.778
How could it? Could it be, Chris?

259
00:22:12.057 --> 00:22:14.704
It's been locked in the
trunk room for years.

260
00:22:16.917 --> 00:22:18.517
It wasn't a nightmare, I tell you.

261
00:22:18.597 --> 00:22:20.017
I saw those things.

262
00:22:20.337 --> 00:22:22.877
I think it was Paul, and
he doesn't want me to marry.

263
00:22:22.957 --> 00:22:26.437
You've got some feeling that it's
not loyal to Paul if you marry again.

264
00:22:26.517 --> 00:22:29.341
It's on your conscience,
and it makes you dream.

265
00:22:29.517 --> 00:22:31.597
Why don't you talk it
over with an analyst?

266
00:22:31.677 --> 00:22:34.657
He wouldn't understand.
He'd think I was losing my mind.

267
00:22:34.737 --> 00:22:39.677
Nobody would understand,
except perhaps Alexis.

268
00:22:40.617 --> 00:22:42.457
Just somebody I once met.

269
00:22:42.537 --> 00:22:43.917
Come on.

270
00:22:46.357 --> 00:22:47.817
Get to bed.

271
00:22:49.997 --> 00:22:53.938
It's after three. You haven't got
time for any more dreams tonight.

272
00:22:57.737 --> 00:22:58.797
Janet.

273
00:22:59.997 --> 00:23:02.457
While you're up, would you...

274
00:23:03.157 --> 00:23:05.177
get me a glass of water?

275
00:23:15.557 --> 00:23:16.477
Thanks.

276
00:23:16.557 --> 00:23:17.737
Janet.

277
00:23:18.737 --> 00:23:21.857
I thought I was supposed to
be the baby in the family.

278
00:23:21.937 --> 00:23:23.797
Here, give me that water.

279
00:23:24.257 --> 00:23:25.637
Move over.

280
00:23:25.777 --> 00:23:26.957
Baby.

281
00:23:30.077 --> 00:23:31.557
Night, Chris.

282
00:23:31.877 --> 00:23:32.957
Night.

283
00:23:43.797 --> 00:23:45.977
Thank you.

284
00:24:26.297 --> 00:24:28.557
Thank you.

285
00:24:36.537 --> 00:24:40.037
Ha, ha, ha, ha, ha, ha.

286
00:25:00.257 --> 00:25:01.817
Childish, isn't it?

287
00:25:02.077 --> 00:25:05.737
I think the ocean and the
fog were more effective.

288
00:25:07.417 --> 00:25:08.697
I agree.

289
00:25:08.777 --> 00:25:12.317
But in my work, I deal with
many different kinds of minds.

290
00:25:12.397 --> 00:25:16.077
Generally speaking, I think you could say
that three types of people come here to

291
00:25:16.157 --> 00:25:16.897
see me.

292
00:25:16.977 --> 00:25:20.897
The first, a fairly large
group, come here to scoff.

293
00:25:21.717 --> 00:25:24.097
Sometimes a few of them remain to pray.

294
00:25:24.657 --> 00:25:26.037
The second,

295
00:25:26.317 --> 00:25:28.417
have childlike, credulous minds.

296
00:25:28.497 --> 00:25:29.677
They long to believe.

297
00:25:29.757 --> 00:25:33.657
They're tired and sad and need comfort.

298
00:25:34.277 --> 00:25:35.877
All this helps them.

299
00:25:35.957 --> 00:25:39.397
It creates an atmosphere.

300
00:25:39.717 --> 00:25:40.697
You see?

301
00:25:40.777 --> 00:25:42.757
A mood, like music.

302
00:25:42.897 --> 00:25:46.837
And in it, they find a few brief moments
of comfort, which helps them to continue

303
00:25:46.917 --> 00:25:49.057
with their gray little lives.

304
00:25:49.557 --> 00:25:52.377
And so I do not think it
is so childish after all.

305
00:25:52.457 --> 00:25:53.257
Do you?

306
00:25:53.337 --> 00:25:54.697
And the third group?

307
00:25:54.777 --> 00:25:55.777
Ah.

308
00:25:56.197 --> 00:26:01.657
The third group is those of us who honestly
explore the secrets of the outer world.

309
00:26:03.157 --> 00:26:06.717
I feel that you have come
here today to join that group.

310
00:26:07.197 --> 00:26:10.477
Well, I... I did want to
talk to you about something.

311
00:26:10.557 --> 00:26:11.557
I know.

312
00:26:12.097 --> 00:26:14.357
He was close to you last night.

313
00:26:16.017 --> 00:26:17.997
And he's close to you now.

314
00:26:18.097 --> 00:26:19.297
But wait.

315
00:26:20.257 --> 00:26:24.197
Maybe I should repel him,
not bring him closer.

316
00:26:24.537 --> 00:26:25.537
Why?

317
00:26:26.397 --> 00:26:29.117
His presence is so strong, it...

318
00:26:29.197 --> 00:26:30.537
it might be dangerous.

319
00:26:30.617 --> 00:26:34.437
How could Paul's presence
possibly be dangerous to me?

320
00:26:34.797 --> 00:26:36.317
To your plans.

321
00:26:37.157 --> 00:26:43.077
You want to get married and lead
what is termed a normal life?

322
00:26:43.337 --> 00:26:46.897
But if Paul is trying to reach
me, then I want to help him.

323
00:26:47.897 --> 00:26:50.250
Have you thought what people will think?

324
00:26:50.757 --> 00:26:52.637
What Martin will think?

325
00:26:53.257 --> 00:26:55.577
I'm not afraid to think as I choose.

326
00:26:57.417 --> 00:27:00.797
Then, Christine, if you have the
courage and the will to explore,

327
00:27:00.877 --> 00:27:02.457
I will help you.

328
00:27:02.777 --> 00:27:04.117
Now relax.

329
00:27:04.657 --> 00:27:08.217
Let your mind be free and receptive.

330
00:27:13.697 --> 00:27:16.377
Janet, you do have a
tendency to dramatize and...

331
00:27:16.457 --> 00:27:18.257
Stop treating me like a child.

332
00:27:18.337 --> 00:27:21.572
I tell you she's been going
to see him for weeks and...

333
00:27:25.557 --> 00:27:26.977
Now, do you believe me?

334
00:27:27.057 --> 00:27:28.117
Mm-mm.

335
00:27:32.777 --> 00:27:35.257
She's been reading psychic books, too.

336
00:27:35.337 --> 00:27:38.177
Talking about Paul as
if he were still alive.

337
00:27:38.637 --> 00:27:40.497
Gives me the creeps.

338
00:27:40.817 --> 00:27:41.877
Let's go.

339
00:27:41.957 --> 00:27:44.877
Now what are we gonna do? We're gonna
get a detective, see what he can find out

340
00:27:44.957 --> 00:27:47.437
about this, this spook chaser.

341
00:27:48.597 --> 00:27:53.217
For 20 years, I've been operating among
these phony mediums as a private detective.

342
00:27:53.297 --> 00:27:57.477
And I've yet to find one concrete bit of
evidence of communication with the dead.

343
00:27:57.557 --> 00:27:59.977
But my sister's absolutely convinced.

344
00:28:00.057 --> 00:28:00.557
He's told me that he's been a detective.

345
00:28:00.617 --> 00:28:03.357
He told her things he couldn't
know, unless he were psychic.

346
00:28:03.437 --> 00:28:06.377
He's probably gone to a lot of
trouble to investigate her past.

347
00:28:06.457 --> 00:28:07.957
That's thorough and clever.

348
00:28:08.037 --> 00:28:11.817
What I can't understand is
Christine falling for such a dribble.

349
00:28:13.017 --> 00:28:15.193
You'd be amazed at the people who do.

350
00:28:15.717 --> 00:28:16.777
No, Pets.

351
00:28:16.897 --> 00:28:20.697
There's a millionaire right in this town
wouldn't buy a bag of popcorn without

352
00:28:20.777 --> 00:28:22.357
consulting his medium.

353
00:28:23.217 --> 00:28:24.637
What's this one's name?

354
00:28:24.717 --> 00:28:25.717
Alexis.

355
00:28:28.257 --> 00:28:30.017
It's a new one on me.

356
00:28:30.497 --> 00:28:32.968
Then they change their names often enough.

357
00:28:33.937 --> 00:28:35.417
How about a description?

358
00:28:36.937 --> 00:28:39.937
There's a picture of him
in this pamphlet he wrote.

359
00:28:45.157 --> 00:28:46.317
Oh, this?

360
00:28:46.517 --> 00:28:48.277
I used to be a magician.

361
00:28:48.537 --> 00:28:50.537
That's how I got into this record.

362
00:29:01.857 --> 00:29:03.777
I know all their tricks.

363
00:29:07.157 --> 00:29:08.157
Alexis.

364
00:29:10.477 --> 00:29:12.837
He was called to Chicago in a case once.

365
00:29:12.917 --> 00:29:18.617
A young man named Maru swindled a society
lady out of a whole hat full of money.

366
00:29:19.097 --> 00:29:21.137
That's Alexis. He certainly looked corny.

367
00:29:21.217 --> 00:29:24.037
If that's Alexis, we can
report him to the police.

368
00:29:24.117 --> 00:29:27.017
What charge? He served a
stretch and that's that.

369
00:29:27.757 --> 00:29:29.717
At least Christine will
be through with him.

370
00:29:29.797 --> 00:29:31.497
I mean, if we can prove he's a crook.

371
00:29:31.577 --> 00:29:33.097
How can we identify him?

372
00:29:33.177 --> 00:29:36.648
Well, I could go out there and
try to get his fingerprints.

373
00:29:36.817 --> 00:29:41.737
But I think the only time these fakes are
really psychic is when there's a detective

374
00:29:41.817 --> 00:29:42.817
around.

375
00:29:43.097 --> 00:29:46.450
They, uh, they look in their
crystals and see you coming.

376
00:29:46.537 --> 00:29:49.157
Maybe we could send
somebody you wouldn't know.

377
00:29:49.237 --> 00:29:51.377
Oh, no, I think that...

378
00:30:08.077 --> 00:30:11.377
Thank you.

379
00:30:37.157 --> 00:30:38.357
No!

380
00:31:13.317 --> 00:31:15.377
Thank you.

381
00:32:37.117 --> 00:32:38.737
Your name, please.

382
00:32:39.377 --> 00:32:40.397
Harper.

383
00:32:40.497 --> 00:32:42.337
Mrs. Charles Harper.

384
00:32:42.417 --> 00:32:43.957
Not Mrs. Charles.

385
00:32:44.037 --> 00:32:45.757
G. Harper. Yes.

386
00:32:46.257 --> 00:32:47.697
I mean, no.

387
00:32:59.077 --> 00:33:03.477
Now concentrate and soon your
thoughts will merge with mine.

388
00:33:04.717 --> 00:33:08.077
Perhaps both hands would be better.

389
00:33:09.037 --> 00:33:10.337
Yes.

390
00:33:12.237 --> 00:33:14.117
So much better.

391
00:33:14.237 --> 00:33:18.017
I can feel your vibrations
much more clearly now.

392
00:33:18.557 --> 00:33:20.077
Oh, but...

393
00:33:20.157 --> 00:33:22.997
But your pulse is racing.

394
00:33:23.437 --> 00:33:25.177
Are you excited?

395
00:33:25.257 --> 00:33:26.437
Nervous?

396
00:33:26.617 --> 00:33:27.617
No.

397
00:33:27.697 --> 00:33:30.717
Oh, but of course, this
problem is troubling you.

398
00:33:31.397 --> 00:33:34.697
The question you wrote comes to me, but...

399
00:33:34.777 --> 00:33:40.597
but faintly, almost as
though it were false.

400
00:33:43.897 --> 00:33:48.837
But another question comes through with
all the power and strength of love and

401
00:33:48.917 --> 00:33:49.917
truth.

402
00:33:50.077 --> 00:33:52.797
You are concerned about someone you love.

403
00:33:52.877 --> 00:33:53.857
A woman.

404
00:33:53.937 --> 00:33:56.217
Your sister. Is it your sister?

405
00:33:56.597 --> 00:33:57.697
Yes.

406
00:33:58.017 --> 00:34:03.117
Older than you, but still you have a
maternal protective feeling about her.

407
00:34:03.477 --> 00:34:06.437
In many ways, you are
more mature than she.

408
00:34:07.537 --> 00:34:08.637
Golly,

409
00:34:08.897 --> 00:34:11.457
I'm glad somebody finally realized that.

410
00:34:11.537 --> 00:34:14.217
Of course, you didn't
always feel that way.

411
00:34:14.337 --> 00:34:16.117
Once you were jealous of her.

412
00:34:16.197 --> 00:34:19.157
You envied her popularity
in her pretty clothes.

413
00:34:19.237 --> 00:34:21.757
And you used to try them
on when she went out.

414
00:34:21.837 --> 00:34:24.297
And they suited you because
there is an air of...

415
00:34:24.377 --> 00:34:27.437
of maturity and sophistication about you.

416
00:34:27.857 --> 00:34:32.957
In many ways, especially intellectually,
you are much older than the many young men

417
00:34:33.037 --> 00:34:34.117
who admire you.

418
00:34:34.197 --> 00:34:36.177
You know, I'd notice that.

419
00:34:37.537 --> 00:34:41.237
Does this feel uncomfortable to you?

420
00:34:42.037 --> 00:34:43.037
I...

421
00:34:44.557 --> 00:34:46.577
I think I'll have a cigarette.

422
00:34:49.237 --> 00:34:50.837
Mrs. Harper?

423
00:34:55.057 --> 00:34:56.737
Mrs. Harper?

424
00:34:59.037 --> 00:35:00.397
May I?

425
00:35:26.597 --> 00:35:29.297
What an attractive case.

426
00:35:48.157 --> 00:35:50.217
Did you know why I wanted them?

427
00:35:50.597 --> 00:35:51.597
No.

428
00:35:52.257 --> 00:35:53.757
Nor do I need to.

429
00:35:53.837 --> 00:35:56.719
It is enough for me to
know that you wanted them.

430
00:36:05.657 --> 00:36:09.597
Alexis, I hope that nothing you've done to
help my sister will get you into trouble

431
00:36:09.677 --> 00:36:10.777
with the police.

432
00:36:10.857 --> 00:36:14.697
I mean, if they should
investigate you or your past life,

433
00:36:14.797 --> 00:36:17.957
will they find anything that
would make trouble for you?

434
00:36:18.037 --> 00:36:19.037
Well,

435
00:36:19.817 --> 00:36:23.817
there are... there are many things
in my life of which I'm ashamed.

436
00:36:24.977 --> 00:36:27.517
But this may sound strange.

437
00:36:28.237 --> 00:36:32.077
The memory that I cherish
is a term in prison.

438
00:36:32.157 --> 00:36:33.157
Oh.

439
00:36:33.857 --> 00:36:38.617
There are many brave men who were imprisoned
in their fight for science and truth,

440
00:36:38.697 --> 00:36:41.017
and I'm proud to be one of them.

441
00:36:42.077 --> 00:36:46.337
I think you may be
able to understand this.

442
00:36:46.777 --> 00:36:48.277
Oh, but anyway,

443
00:36:48.597 --> 00:36:50.517
do with this as you think best.

444
00:36:50.597 --> 00:36:51.797
I trust you.

445
00:37:11.957 --> 00:37:16.677
Ah, ah, ah, ah, ah, ah, ah, ah.

446
00:37:42.357 --> 00:37:43.717
Hey, Janet.

447
00:37:44.537 --> 00:37:46.117
Oh, hello, Martin.

448
00:37:46.197 --> 00:37:47.557
Did you get the fingerprints?

449
00:37:47.637 --> 00:37:48.877
Yes. Good.

450
00:37:48.957 --> 00:37:50.477
I erased them.

451
00:37:50.897 --> 00:37:55.477
Oh, Martin, he isn't at all the
kind of a man we thought he was.

452
00:38:14.857 --> 00:38:16.017
Thank you.

453
00:38:23.057 --> 00:38:24.217
Oh, hello, dear.

454
00:38:24.297 --> 00:38:25.757
Did you get the day off?

455
00:38:25.837 --> 00:38:28.017
Not time to. Yeah, Mrs.

456
00:38:28.097 --> 00:38:30.117
Faber. No, Mrs. Faber.

457
00:38:30.397 --> 00:38:32.057
I'm getting sick of it.

458
00:38:32.377 --> 00:38:34.017
And how is our client?

459
00:38:34.097 --> 00:38:37.397
She's hooked good. Mm, good she is, huh?

460
00:38:37.537 --> 00:38:40.837
And, uh, must you feel that her
beloved husband is close to her?

461
00:38:40.917 --> 00:38:45.137
Surprised she hasn't asked me to lay
an extra plate for the dinner table.

462
00:38:45.217 --> 00:38:46.917
Here's a photograph of her parents.

463
00:38:46.997 --> 00:38:49.157
It was in the trunk where
I found the wedding dress.

464
00:38:49.237 --> 00:38:51.997
Oh, that would be very useful.

465
00:38:54.457 --> 00:38:56.137
When are you gonna put the bite on her?

466
00:38:56.217 --> 00:38:59.117
Oh, Vivian, you put things so crudely.

467
00:38:59.197 --> 00:39:01.177
I imagine that very soon Mrs.

468
00:39:01.257 --> 00:39:04.617
Faber is going to press money on
me to continue my psychic research.

469
00:39:04.697 --> 00:39:06.817
Good. I'm getting sick
of this Swedish egg.

470
00:39:06.897 --> 00:39:09.957
I need a new role. Oh,
I have a new assignment for you.

471
00:39:10.037 --> 00:39:12.197
Straight from the obituary column.

472
00:39:12.277 --> 00:39:14.397
A lonely millionaire who...

473
00:39:16.217 --> 00:39:18.277
I had no more appointments today.

474
00:39:18.657 --> 00:39:19.717
Oh, here.

475
00:39:20.357 --> 00:39:22.617
Help me get these things out of the way.

476
00:39:22.697 --> 00:39:25.057
What do you pay for those
chunks of glass these days?

477
00:39:25.137 --> 00:39:26.517
Three dollars apiece.

478
00:39:26.597 --> 00:39:28.157
Used to be a dollar ninety-five.

479
00:39:28.237 --> 00:39:31.797
You know, somebody ought to do
something about this high cost of living.

480
00:39:31.877 --> 00:39:37.237
You should complain. You're selling them
for 25 bucks to your psychic students.

481
00:39:37.317 --> 00:39:40.357
I used to, but who am
I to fight inflation?

482
00:39:40.517 --> 00:39:42.497
I'm charging 50 now.

483
00:39:45.917 --> 00:39:47.217
Expensive car.

484
00:39:49.777 --> 00:39:51.097
Expensive clothes.

485
00:39:51.177 --> 00:39:54.297
I would say no brains and lots of money.

486
00:39:54.377 --> 00:39:57.017
So long as you're here, Vivian, you
might as well check the car for me.

487
00:39:57.097 --> 00:40:00.077
There might be some papers or
something in the glove compartment.

488
00:40:00.157 --> 00:40:01.157
Okay.

489
00:40:01.237 --> 00:40:04.177
I'll use a turban in case
you find out something.

490
00:40:35.977 --> 00:40:38.777
Thank you.

491
00:41:02.457 --> 00:41:03.797
This is such a pleasure.

492
00:41:03.877 --> 00:41:05.837
I mean, I've heard...

493
00:41:25.037 --> 00:41:26.497
I'd rather not tonight, Martin.

494
00:41:26.577 --> 00:41:28.197
Really, I'm much too tired.

495
00:41:30.477 --> 00:41:32.597
Well, let's decide about Friday later.

496
00:41:32.677 --> 00:41:33.897
Call me tomorrow.

497
00:41:35.137 --> 00:41:36.277
Good night.

498
00:41:36.797 --> 00:41:38.717
Will that be all, Mrs. Favre?

499
00:41:40.517 --> 00:41:42.277
That's all, Emily. Thanks.

500
00:41:49.517 --> 00:41:50.437
Chris.

501
00:41:50.517 --> 00:41:53.223
Hmm? Let you and I spend
the evening together.

502
00:41:53.457 --> 00:41:55.217
If you're tired, we could go to a movie.

503
00:41:55.297 --> 00:41:56.837
Actually, I'm busy tonight, Janet.

504
00:41:56.917 --> 00:41:58.211
I have an appointment.

505
00:41:58.917 --> 00:42:01.757
Chris, don't see Alexis tonight.

506
00:42:01.837 --> 00:42:03.857
Don't see him ever again.

507
00:42:04.077 --> 00:42:06.417
Janet, I thought we
agreed not to discuss this.

508
00:42:06.497 --> 00:42:07.697
I'm not attacking Alexis.

509
00:42:07.777 --> 00:42:11.497
Honest. I think he'd be
very good for certain people.

510
00:42:11.577 --> 00:42:13.157
But not for me.

511
00:42:13.517 --> 00:42:18.057
Well, you see, when some people
meet a fascinating personality,

512
00:42:18.137 --> 00:42:20.157
they're swept right off their feet.

513
00:42:20.237 --> 00:42:21.577
Like me?

514
00:42:21.817 --> 00:42:22.937
Yes.

515
00:42:23.497 --> 00:42:27.317
But other people can be
more objective, like me.

516
00:42:28.077 --> 00:42:29.657
It's true, Chris.

517
00:42:29.737 --> 00:42:33.077
In many ways, I'm more
mature than you are.

518
00:42:33.157 --> 00:42:37.377
Janet, I have lived through most of your
growing pains, and oftentimes you've been

519
00:42:37.457 --> 00:42:38.837
insufferable, but tonight this is...

520
00:42:38.917 --> 00:42:40.837
You see? You can't be objective.

521
00:42:40.917 --> 00:42:44.237
You're getting sore just because I'm
trying to give you some good advice.

522
00:42:44.317 --> 00:42:45.677
Give me advice?

523
00:42:45.757 --> 00:42:47.777
Janet, please. It is good advice.

524
00:42:47.857 --> 00:42:49.597
Alexis agrees with me.

525
00:42:50.577 --> 00:42:52.497
Alexis agrees with you?

526
00:42:54.037 --> 00:42:55.737
You see,

527
00:42:56.037 --> 00:43:00.277
I've talked to him a few
times about your problem.

528
00:43:01.537 --> 00:43:04.977
I... I went to his house.

529
00:43:05.297 --> 00:43:07.577
How did you find him?

530
00:43:10.037 --> 00:43:13.437
Fascinating. Absolutely fascinating.

531
00:43:14.657 --> 00:43:20.777
You know, he has one of the
most vibrant personalities.

532
00:43:21.997 --> 00:43:24.817
to this conclusion while
discussing my problem?

533
00:43:25.697 --> 00:43:28.597
Well, we talked a little about me, too.

534
00:43:30.157 --> 00:43:31.217
Janet...

535
00:43:31.577 --> 00:43:32.857
Good night.

536
00:43:33.517 --> 00:43:36.877
I'll leave you alone
with your objectivity.

537
00:44:01.257 --> 00:44:03.277
Let's go.

538
00:44:30.817 --> 00:44:32.137
I had to see you.

539
00:44:32.217 --> 00:44:33.757
Don't pay any attention to it.

540
00:44:33.837 --> 00:44:35.457
I feel a disturbance.

541
00:44:35.637 --> 00:44:40.237
If we are to reach the outer world tonight,
we must leave behind us all thoughts

542
00:44:40.317 --> 00:44:44.437
that are hostile and on
a low emotional level.

543
00:44:47.557 --> 00:44:49.837
Now, Janet, this is your first seance.

544
00:44:49.917 --> 00:44:51.917
There must be some
skepticism in your mind.

545
00:44:51.997 --> 00:44:54.597
Oh, no. Perhaps only in
your subconscious mind.

546
00:44:54.677 --> 00:44:57.217
But nevertheless, we shall
proceed under test conditions,

547
00:44:57.297 --> 00:45:00.356
if you would be good enough
to hold this key for me.

548
00:45:00.897 --> 00:45:03.937
And Christine, will you tie
my wrists together securely?

549
00:45:04.017 --> 00:45:05.517
Oh, there's no need to do that, Alexis.

550
00:45:05.597 --> 00:45:06.757
I have every faith in you.

551
00:45:06.837 --> 00:45:08.817
Oh, but I insist, for my own sake.

552
00:45:08.897 --> 00:45:10.257
Here, Chris. You take the key.

553
00:45:10.337 --> 00:45:12.457
I'll tie his wrist. All right, Janet.

554
00:45:12.537 --> 00:45:14.037
Start with the right one.

555
00:45:15.897 --> 00:45:17.697
Tie one knot in the center.

556
00:45:19.057 --> 00:45:20.057
Tightly.

557
00:45:20.617 --> 00:45:21.657
That's good.

558
00:45:21.997 --> 00:45:24.097
I'll tie my hands behind my back.

559
00:45:26.297 --> 00:45:27.697
Very tightly.

560
00:45:31.517 --> 00:45:32.697
Thank you.

561
00:45:35.457 --> 00:45:37.497
Now lock the cabinet...

562
00:45:38.117 --> 00:45:41.705
so that you know that I cannot
possibly move from this chair.

563
00:45:48.097 --> 00:45:50.803
Now, Christine, will
you switch off the light?

564
00:45:56.337 --> 00:45:59.017
The room must be completely dark.

565
00:45:59.957 --> 00:46:01.837
Sit down on the table.

566
00:46:06.157 --> 00:46:11.577
And clasp your hands together
tightly with a key between your palms.

567
00:46:16.257 --> 00:46:22.157
If you see anything at all,
do not move around or cry out.

568
00:46:26.137 --> 00:46:29.037
Thank you.

569
00:47:04.157 --> 00:47:06.417
I don't remember him very well.

570
00:47:06.497 --> 00:47:08.437
Isn't that...

571
00:47:08.897 --> 00:47:10.457
Yes, Janet.

572
00:47:11.697 --> 00:47:12.917
That's Dad.

573
00:47:27.497 --> 00:47:29.577
Do you feel it, Janet?

574
00:47:29.897 --> 00:47:31.137
The chill?

575
00:47:34.457 --> 00:47:38.717
Cold breeze, like ice.

576
00:48:19.277 --> 00:48:21.937
I'd sure like to know
what's going on in there.

577
00:48:22.017 --> 00:48:25.937
If he's staging a seance, it'd be a
good opportunity to prove he's a phony.

578
00:48:26.017 --> 00:48:28.517
Let's sneak around back. There may
be a window we can look through.

579
00:48:28.597 --> 00:48:29.917
Or climb through.

580
00:48:56.917 --> 00:48:57.917
Paul.

581
00:49:17.797 --> 00:49:18.997
Martin, what are you doing?

582
00:49:19.077 --> 00:49:20.597
Showing you what a phony this man is.

583
00:49:20.677 --> 00:49:21.677
Where's the key?

584
00:49:24.537 --> 00:49:26.677
If you think he's still in here...

585
00:49:39.497 --> 00:49:40.697
What is it?

586
00:49:42.337 --> 00:49:44.217
It's quick on his feet,
I'll say that for him.

587
00:49:44.297 --> 00:49:46.137
How could he get out when I locked him in?

588
00:49:46.217 --> 00:49:48.177
He could show you if he wanted to.

589
00:49:48.257 --> 00:49:50.017
You tie a knot like this.

590
00:49:52.977 --> 00:49:54.317
Martin, this is dreadful.

591
00:49:54.397 --> 00:49:55.657
Yes, it is.

592
00:49:55.877 --> 00:49:58.877
I'm afraid I must insist
you don't come here again.

593
00:49:59.137 --> 00:50:01.017
Insist? By what right do you insist?

594
00:50:01.097 --> 00:50:02.477
Oh, we're engaged.

595
00:50:02.737 --> 00:50:05.497
Even if we were married,
I'd think as I pleased.

596
00:50:05.577 --> 00:50:09.117
And I won't close my mind to truth just
because you don't happen to understand them.

597
00:50:09.197 --> 00:50:11.617
Truth, poppycock. Be logical, Chris.

598
00:50:11.697 --> 00:50:12.797
Be logical.

599
00:50:13.157 --> 00:50:17.757
Pardon me, Mr. Abbott. Do you think you
could possibly defer your discussion long

600
00:50:17.837 --> 00:50:19.737
enough to untie this rope?

601
00:50:21.437 --> 00:50:23.957
I happen to understand more
about these things than you do.

602
00:50:24.037 --> 00:50:26.017
Do you realize that man
has a criminal record?

603
00:50:26.097 --> 00:50:28.917
Alexis told us he was once in
prison, if that's what you mean.

604
00:50:28.997 --> 00:50:31.557
Sometimes a prison record
is something to be proud of.

605
00:50:31.637 --> 00:50:34.257
I'm terribly sorry about
this interruption, Alexis.

606
00:50:34.337 --> 00:50:36.857
I saw him. I saw Paul.

607
00:50:36.937 --> 00:50:39.897
We will bring him back. Even
if you could bring him back,

608
00:50:39.977 --> 00:50:42.417
she'd be better off if he
stayed where he is, dead.

609
00:50:42.497 --> 00:50:44.417
She never meant anything to him but money.

610
00:50:44.497 --> 00:50:47.997
Martin. He'd been married and divorced
before, each time with cash settlements.

611
00:50:48.077 --> 00:50:52.797
If he'd lived, he'd have made you
miserable and ended up where he belongs,

612
00:50:52.877 --> 00:50:56.657
in jail. I never thought you'd attack
a dead man with lies he can't answer.

613
00:50:56.737 --> 00:50:58.397
Yes, it's in the records.

614
00:50:58.557 --> 00:51:02.317
When Martin disappeared, did you or
did you not ask me to investigate?

615
00:51:02.397 --> 00:51:06.557
Yes. Well, in the course of my
snooping, I found a second Mrs.

616
00:51:06.637 --> 00:51:07.617
Faber.

617
00:51:07.697 --> 00:51:10.757
Divorced in Las Vegas just six
months before he married you.

618
00:51:10.837 --> 00:51:13.072
I think he went over there to see her.

619
00:51:13.377 --> 00:51:17.257
I never could find her,
though, and Paul was dead, so...

620
00:51:18.077 --> 00:51:20.797
But if it were true, you didn't
find it necessary to tell Mrs.

621
00:51:20.877 --> 00:51:21.657
Fever.

622
00:51:21.737 --> 00:51:23.757
Yes, I suppose it is strange.

623
00:51:24.757 --> 00:51:27.717
Very silly to try and
protect the girl you love.

624
00:51:28.857 --> 00:51:32.417
She only runs as fast as she
can to another phony like you.

625
00:51:33.697 --> 00:51:35.937
Come on, Hoffman, let's get out of here.

626
00:51:36.017 --> 00:51:38.977
Going so soon? I hope we
might stay around a while.

627
00:51:39.057 --> 00:51:40.077
You should stay.

628
00:51:40.157 --> 00:51:42.717
We'd soon learn what
amazing powers Alexis has.

629
00:51:42.797 --> 00:51:44.517
I'm always willing to learn.

630
00:51:45.017 --> 00:51:47.377
Perhaps you'd like to resume the seance.

631
00:51:49.077 --> 00:51:51.557
Under real test conditions.

632
00:51:51.777 --> 00:51:54.797
And have you report me
for conducting a seance?

633
00:51:55.317 --> 00:51:56.877
I should say not.

634
00:51:56.957 --> 00:51:59.477
He probably could report you now.

635
00:51:59.557 --> 00:52:04.017
But if you'll sit down here at this table,
and while we all hold hands like real

636
00:52:04.097 --> 00:52:07.957
good friends, if you can make
any of your spirits walk or talk,

637
00:52:08.037 --> 00:52:10.617
I think Mr. Hoffman will
let it ride this time.

638
00:52:10.697 --> 00:52:12.117
It's ridiculous.

639
00:52:12.197 --> 00:52:13.617
Show them, Alexis.

640
00:52:13.697 --> 00:52:15.037
Yes, Alexis.

641
00:52:15.617 --> 00:52:16.717
Do.

642
00:52:18.077 --> 00:52:21.097
In hostile circumstances, any contact
with the spirit world would be practically

643
00:52:21.177 --> 00:52:22.017
impossible.

644
00:52:22.097 --> 00:52:24.917
Yeah, yeah. I've heard
that pattern routine before.

645
00:52:24.997 --> 00:52:27.497
I know it's easier when you can
run around the room in the dark.

646
00:52:27.577 --> 00:52:29.897
It doesn't run around in the dark.

647
00:52:30.117 --> 00:52:34.097
Go on, Alexis. Let them see
for themselves what you can do.

648
00:52:34.837 --> 00:52:36.657
Okay, let's go.

649
00:52:51.057 --> 00:52:52.057
Huh?

650
00:52:54.737 --> 00:52:58.217
You know, you really ought to have an
assistant on the premises for tight spots like

651
00:52:58.297 --> 00:52:59.137
this.

652
00:52:59.217 --> 00:53:01.537
We checked, you see,
to make sure you hadn't.

653
00:53:01.617 --> 00:53:03.277
Shh, if he talks so loud.

654
00:53:03.357 --> 00:53:05.617
You mean we'll frighten the ghosts?

655
00:53:47.297 --> 00:53:48.357
Christine.

656
00:53:50.157 --> 00:53:51.657
It's Paul.

657
00:53:52.577 --> 00:53:54.217
It's his voice.

658
00:53:54.357 --> 00:53:56.717
I didn't mean to frighten you ever.

659
00:53:57.197 --> 00:53:59.557
But I had to get through to you somehow.

660
00:54:00.237 --> 00:54:02.717
I've always loved you, you know that.

661
00:54:03.177 --> 00:54:04.497
Yes, Paul.

662
00:54:11.317 --> 00:54:15.957
And if you could only believe,
we could be together for always.

663
00:54:32.317 --> 00:54:33.837
What the hell?

664
00:54:33.917 --> 00:54:34.457
That's not on the moment.

665
00:54:34.537 --> 00:54:36.137
You're not on the mind.

666
00:54:36.217 --> 00:54:37.570
You're not on the mind.

667
00:54:38.497 --> 00:54:40.837
You're on the mind.

668
00:54:40.917 --> 00:54:42.577
You're on the mind.

669
00:54:47.797 --> 00:54:49.237
I'll take you home.

670
00:55:29.917 --> 00:55:31.757
What are you so startled about?

671
00:55:31.837 --> 00:55:34.425
You're used to raising
the dead, aren't you?

672
00:55:34.737 --> 00:55:37.077
Although I guess with me
it was a little easier.

673
00:55:37.157 --> 00:55:39.377
Because Hop only legally dead.

674
00:55:41.277 --> 00:55:45.697
You know, I've been watching you ever
since my widow started coming to you,

675
00:55:45.777 --> 00:55:46.477
Alexis.

676
00:55:46.557 --> 00:55:48.910
I find your methods quite similar to me.

677
00:55:53.597 --> 00:55:55.017
Mmm. Excellent.

678
00:56:02.257 --> 00:56:03.257
The rope thing.

679
00:56:05.317 --> 00:56:06.317
Valiperation.

680
00:56:06.777 --> 00:56:08.601
But how did you... The cabinet?

681
00:56:13.757 --> 00:56:16.697
Just push a button and the
idiots reach for their checkbooks.

682
00:56:16.777 --> 00:56:18.957
It isn't quite as simple as all that.

683
00:56:19.237 --> 00:56:22.178
There's a lot of psychology
involved and research.

684
00:56:22.417 --> 00:56:24.377
Sounds dangerously like work.

685
00:56:25.797 --> 00:56:29.856
However, your bag of tricks can be
useful to us in our present plans.

686
00:56:30.197 --> 00:56:32.797
We, uh, have plans.

687
00:56:34.117 --> 00:56:37.157
I didn't help you out tonight
just for the fun of it, my friend.

688
00:56:37.237 --> 00:56:38.537
I can use you.

689
00:56:39.277 --> 00:56:40.817
You see, I need money.

690
00:56:41.017 --> 00:56:42.337
A lot of it.

691
00:56:42.937 --> 00:56:44.437
I don't understand.

692
00:56:45.237 --> 00:56:48.437
If you went back to Christine,
you could have anything you want.

693
00:56:48.517 --> 00:56:50.757
You don't seem to have
a very analytical mind.

694
00:56:50.837 --> 00:56:52.857
Oh, but Christine still loves you.

695
00:56:53.197 --> 00:56:54.617
She... Oh.

696
00:56:55.517 --> 00:56:58.917
You mean if you weren't dead, the
police might want to know who it was

697
00:56:58.997 --> 00:57:00.437
death in your car, hmm?

698
00:57:00.517 --> 00:57:01.517
Exactly.

699
00:57:04.577 --> 00:57:06.777
Have I been using the
wrong piece of music?

700
00:57:06.857 --> 00:57:07.617
What?

701
00:57:07.697 --> 00:57:08.817
Oh, no, no.

702
00:57:08.897 --> 00:57:11.073
You have the right one for Christine.

703
00:57:11.537 --> 00:57:13.877
I used this one for my first wife.

704
00:57:15.657 --> 00:57:17.951
Was it her ashes they found in the car?

705
00:57:18.957 --> 00:57:21.257
It was me they found in the car.

706
00:57:21.337 --> 00:57:22.737
Don't forget it.

707
00:57:24.077 --> 00:57:27.783
Perhaps I should point out that
I'm in a very curious position.

708
00:57:28.997 --> 00:57:30.657
I could even kill a man.

709
00:57:30.737 --> 00:57:32.617
Nobody think of looking for me.

710
00:57:33.557 --> 00:57:35.969
You're in a curious position too, Alexis.

711
00:57:36.197 --> 00:57:39.997
You can't tell anybody I'm
alive without exposing yourself.

712
00:57:40.077 --> 00:57:41.517
That's right, I can't.

713
00:57:41.597 --> 00:57:43.457
It is a very curious position.

714
00:57:43.537 --> 00:57:45.297
And a profitable one.

715
00:57:46.097 --> 00:57:50.257
I don't think it's too premature to
congratulate you upon an extremely advantageous

716
00:57:50.337 --> 00:57:51.337
marriage.

717
00:57:52.037 --> 00:57:53.057
Marriage?

718
00:57:53.137 --> 00:57:54.597
Come, come, my friend.

719
00:57:54.677 --> 00:57:57.677
You must be prepared to
make some small sacrifices.

720
00:57:59.417 --> 00:58:01.797
No, no, the little one, Janet.

721
00:58:02.917 --> 00:58:05.623
She's still young enough
to be dazzled by you.

722
00:58:06.497 --> 00:58:10.917
From then on, getting that estate will
be like taking candy away from a baby.

723
00:58:10.997 --> 00:58:13.297
I don't think Christine would allow it.

724
00:58:14.177 --> 00:58:15.997
I'll take care of Christine.

725
00:58:21.897 --> 00:58:23.457
I'm a little rusty on this one.

726
00:58:23.537 --> 00:58:24.897
I'll have to practice.

727
00:58:26.477 --> 00:58:28.477
Have this piano tuned, will you?

728
00:58:50.677 --> 00:58:52.877
Oh, no, ma'am, not for you.

729
00:58:52.957 --> 00:58:55.417
Not after the night. You've been happy.

730
00:58:56.477 --> 00:58:58.497
Yes, that's for you. Hot milk?

731
00:58:58.577 --> 00:59:01.237
Yes, ma'am. I'd rather have the coffee.

732
00:59:05.477 --> 00:59:07.536
I'll see that she drinks it, Emily.

733
00:59:09.597 --> 00:59:11.857
All right, I'll take it,
but it won't do any good.

734
00:59:11.937 --> 00:59:14.877
You should really try and
get more sleep, Christine.

735
00:59:14.957 --> 00:59:16.837
But I just can't.

736
00:59:17.557 --> 00:59:18.997
I'm never alone anymore.

737
00:59:19.077 --> 00:59:19.957
I...

738
00:59:20.037 --> 00:59:23.817
I keep hearing his voice
now and seeing his face.

739
00:59:25.097 --> 00:59:28.568
It should comfort me, I suppose,
but it's been frightening.

740
00:59:29.597 --> 00:59:31.117
Drink your milk while it's hot.

741
00:59:31.197 --> 00:59:32.297
It'll help.

742
00:59:34.797 --> 00:59:37.397
Alexis, do you suppose
we've made a mistake?

743
00:59:37.957 --> 00:59:41.777
That first day when I came to your house,
you said it might be better to repel

744
00:59:41.857 --> 00:59:44.445
Paul's spirit instead
of bringing it closer.

745
00:59:45.297 --> 00:59:46.817
Perhaps you were right.

746
00:59:47.377 --> 00:59:48.977
Perhaps I was right.

747
00:59:50.037 --> 00:59:51.997
Try to break the contact now and not go...

748
00:59:52.077 --> 00:59:53.217
It's too late.

749
00:59:53.357 --> 00:59:56.122
At this stage, I could
never break the contact.

750
00:59:56.857 --> 00:59:59.033
We'll have to finish what we started.

751
00:59:59.177 --> 01:00:01.597
If it would only bring me rest.

752
01:00:04.237 --> 01:00:05.557
Well, let's...

753
01:00:05.637 --> 01:00:07.477
let's begin the seance now.

754
01:00:09.557 --> 01:00:13.637
I think that before we
start, you should relax.

755
01:00:13.897 --> 01:00:16.956
And I should be more familiar
with the surroundings.

756
01:00:17.237 --> 01:00:20.057
It is very difficult for
me in a strange house.

757
01:00:21.477 --> 01:00:23.817
Perhaps if I walked around
the outside for a while...

758
01:00:23.897 --> 01:00:26.426
I'll show you. We can
even go to the ocean.

759
01:00:26.997 --> 01:00:29.497
Go ahead, Jan. I'm feeling rather tired.

760
01:01:00.357 --> 01:01:02.837
Watch your step. It's awfully steep.

761
01:01:33.377 --> 01:01:36.457
I think I have talent along spirit lines.

762
01:01:36.557 --> 01:01:37.557
You do?

763
01:01:37.637 --> 01:01:38.317
Yes.

764
01:01:38.397 --> 01:01:40.017
I've been reading and studying.

765
01:01:40.097 --> 01:01:41.897
I think maybe I'm mediumistic.

766
01:01:41.977 --> 01:01:42.917
Oh.

767
01:01:42.997 --> 01:01:45.757
You know that seance we had at
your house when Paul appeared?

768
01:01:45.837 --> 01:01:46.657
Yes.

769
01:01:46.737 --> 01:01:48.897
Was that Paul's first visitation?

770
01:01:51.317 --> 01:01:53.137
Oh, yes, it was.

771
01:01:53.217 --> 01:01:57.817
You see, maybe it's because I was
there, all tuned in and sensitive.

772
01:01:58.557 --> 01:02:00.177
Well, it could be.

773
01:02:00.257 --> 01:02:03.022
Did you ever see a better
spirit manifestation?

774
01:02:04.057 --> 01:02:05.297
No, darling.

775
01:02:05.377 --> 01:02:07.737
That was an all-time record.

776
01:02:20.257 --> 01:02:22.697
Are you thinking about my sister?

777
01:02:23.157 --> 01:02:24.157
Yes.

778
01:02:24.597 --> 01:02:26.997
She's very attractive, isn't she?

779
01:02:28.097 --> 01:02:30.457
Most men admire Chris.

780
01:02:30.617 --> 01:02:33.797
But Christine will always belong to Paul.

781
01:02:34.237 --> 01:02:35.897
I suppose that's true.

782
01:02:36.897 --> 01:02:39.457
I wish you wouldn't be so unhappy.

783
01:02:41.477 --> 01:02:47.037
Do you suppose they'll be joined together
someday in celestial companionship through

784
01:02:47.117 --> 01:02:48.537
all eternity?

785
01:02:50.357 --> 01:02:52.397
Yes, I'm sure they will.

786
01:02:54.057 --> 01:02:55.097
Alexis.

787
01:02:56.417 --> 01:02:59.557
Do you think I'd make a
good celestial companion?

788
01:03:00.017 --> 01:03:02.057
Darling, I think you'd be wonderful.

789
01:03:02.137 --> 01:03:04.497
And we'd love each other
through all eternity?

790
01:03:04.577 --> 01:03:06.237
Oh, even longer.

791
01:03:07.177 --> 01:03:08.217
Alexis.

792
01:03:08.957 --> 01:03:11.977
Why don't we start right
here on the Earth plane?

793
01:03:30.537 --> 01:03:32.217
Oh, my God.

794
01:03:51.117 --> 01:03:52.277
Thank you.

795
01:04:32.117 --> 01:04:33.777
I've come for you, dear.

796
01:04:41.397 --> 01:04:42.677
Hurry, Chris.

797
01:04:45.037 --> 01:04:46.277
Paul, wait.

798
01:04:48.397 --> 01:04:49.577
I'm frightened.

799
01:04:49.657 --> 01:04:52.245
I'm afraid. There's
nothing to be afraid of.

800
01:05:34.157 --> 01:05:35.857
I'm afraid.

801
01:05:37.937 --> 01:05:39.517
Race you to the beach.

802
01:05:42.977 --> 01:05:44.817
Last one is a coward.

803
01:06:10.397 --> 01:06:12.297
Help!

804
01:07:06.697 --> 01:07:08.577
Alexis, she's not badly hurt.

805
01:07:08.657 --> 01:07:09.897
She's all right?

806
01:07:09.977 --> 01:07:11.717
I think she's all right.

807
01:07:28.197 --> 01:07:29.817
What are you doing here?

808
01:07:29.937 --> 01:07:33.957
Well, at the moment, I'm opening
the door, but not very wide.

809
01:07:34.037 --> 01:07:36.017
You're not exactly welcome here.

810
01:07:36.697 --> 01:07:37.617
Where's Emily?

811
01:07:37.697 --> 01:07:39.257
It's Thursday, Mr. Abbott.

812
01:07:39.337 --> 01:07:40.557
Her day off.

813
01:07:41.417 --> 01:07:42.577
Where's Freda?

814
01:07:42.837 --> 01:07:46.661
Freda left yesterday. She said
she thought the house was haunted.

815
01:07:46.857 --> 01:07:48.617
I can see what she means.

816
01:07:49.017 --> 01:07:50.797
I want to talk to Mrs. Faber.

817
01:07:50.877 --> 01:07:53.171
I don't think she wants to talk to you.

818
01:07:55.237 --> 01:07:56.237
Janet.

819
01:07:57.037 --> 01:07:58.757
Chris. She's mad.

820
01:07:58.837 --> 01:08:01.457
She's making herself ill
with this morbid nonsense.

821
01:08:01.537 --> 01:08:02.817
Oh, it isn't bad.

822
01:08:02.977 --> 01:08:05.389
Chris had a fall last night on the cliff.

823
01:08:07.377 --> 01:08:09.537
What was she doing on the cliff?

824
01:08:10.557 --> 01:08:11.557
Well...

825
01:08:11.777 --> 01:08:14.317
She thought you heard Paul calling.

826
01:08:14.877 --> 01:08:16.037
Janet?

827
01:08:16.937 --> 01:08:19.197
We'll always be together, Chris.

828
01:08:21.697 --> 01:08:22.617
Together.

829
01:08:22.697 --> 01:08:23.777
Always.

830
01:08:24.117 --> 01:08:25.317
Yes, Paul.

831
01:08:26.257 --> 01:08:28.997
You'll always be mine, Chris.

832
01:08:36.697 --> 01:08:39.357
Chris, I'm waiting.

833
01:08:40.917 --> 01:08:42.437
I know.

834
01:08:44.637 --> 01:08:46.397
I know.

835
01:08:46.797 --> 01:08:48.817
We'll be together, Chris.

836
01:08:48.897 --> 01:08:51.057
Together, always.

837
01:08:51.317 --> 01:08:52.317
Together.

838
01:09:05.137 --> 01:09:06.697
What did the doctor say?

839
01:09:07.077 --> 01:09:08.897
Chris wouldn't have a doctor.

840
01:09:09.677 --> 01:09:11.577
You've got to be logical about this.

841
01:09:11.657 --> 01:09:13.157
She's not badly hurt.

842
01:09:13.237 --> 01:09:14.997
She's just bruised and shaken.

843
01:09:15.077 --> 01:09:17.257
In the first place,
she's in a state of hysteria.

844
01:09:17.337 --> 01:09:19.377
In the second place, she
shouldn't be in this house.

845
01:09:19.457 --> 01:09:21.157
Everything in it reminds her of Paul.

846
01:09:21.237 --> 01:09:21.737
And she's not a bad person.

847
01:09:21.657 --> 01:09:23.917
And finally, she shouldn't
be left alone for one second.

848
01:09:23.997 --> 01:09:25.457
There's no telling what she might do.

849
01:09:25.537 --> 01:09:27.137
But I'm with her all the time.

850
01:09:27.217 --> 01:09:28.457
I haven't left her.

851
01:09:32.397 --> 01:09:33.397
Maria!

852
01:09:35.977 --> 01:09:36.977
Chris!

853
01:09:39.197 --> 01:09:40.197
Martin!

854
01:09:59.517 --> 01:10:01.597
Yes, of course.

855
01:10:01.677 --> 01:10:03.217
What's in that bottle?

856
01:10:04.317 --> 01:10:06.157
It's just aspirin.

857
01:10:06.757 --> 01:10:08.577
I've got a headache.

858
01:10:10.677 --> 01:10:13.677
Chris, it's dangerous
for you to stay here.

859
01:10:13.757 --> 01:10:17.177
You need rest and care,
perhaps in a hospital.

860
01:10:17.257 --> 01:10:18.877
You're trying to separate us.

861
01:10:18.957 --> 01:10:20.837
I won't go to a hospital.

862
01:10:20.917 --> 01:10:21.577
I won't leave the hospital.

863
01:10:21.657 --> 01:10:23.157
Only until you're better.

864
01:10:23.237 --> 01:10:24.237
No.

865
01:10:24.717 --> 01:10:27.011
You're trying to drive them away again.

866
01:10:27.257 --> 01:10:28.477
Now get out.

867
01:10:28.557 --> 01:10:29.737
Get out.

868
01:10:34.017 --> 01:10:36.017
I'll leave before I upset her anymore.

869
01:10:36.097 --> 01:10:38.038
I'll see you at the door, Martin.

870
01:10:45.017 --> 01:10:46.457
They've left the room.

871
01:10:46.657 --> 01:10:48.817
Then you can resume the treatment.

872
01:10:48.997 --> 01:10:51.821
If you hadn't tried to
play the hero last night,

873
01:10:52.357 --> 01:10:54.277
I had to save Janet, didn't I?

874
01:10:54.357 --> 01:10:56.797
You're not falling in
love with her, are you?

875
01:10:56.877 --> 01:11:00.517
Look, if I'm going to marry into this estate,
there's got to be somebody for me to

876
01:11:00.597 --> 01:11:01.597
marry.

877
01:11:01.857 --> 01:11:05.837
Just by way of making conversation,
that wife of mine in Las Vegas,

878
01:11:05.917 --> 01:11:09.397
the one who tried to back out on me
and wanted her cash settlement back,

879
01:11:09.477 --> 01:11:12.697
I settled with her, but not for cash.

880
01:11:13.277 --> 01:11:17.097
Now, if you should back out on me,
I wouldn't hesitate to settle with you either,

881
01:11:17.177 --> 01:11:21.057
Alexis. I know, because you're
in such a curious position.

882
01:11:39.957 --> 01:11:42.357
Oh, you're bright.

883
01:11:42.437 --> 01:11:44.157
How long, Chris?

884
01:11:44.237 --> 01:11:46.797
How long must I wait?

885
01:12:11.977 --> 01:12:14.157
Thank you.

886
01:12:59.457 --> 01:13:01.237
Come on. Janet,

887
01:13:01.717 --> 01:13:02.637
get out of here.

888
01:13:02.717 --> 01:13:05.477
Why didn't you tell me you
wanted money that badly?

889
01:13:05.557 --> 01:13:07.797
I've given it to you just to get rid.

890
01:13:09.017 --> 01:13:10.017
Paul.

891
01:13:11.217 --> 01:13:12.877
Yes, I'm alive.

892
01:13:14.197 --> 01:13:16.857
Legally, I'm dead, and
I'm going to stay that way.

893
01:13:16.937 --> 01:13:19.217
If you're alive, I won't be able to.

894
01:13:19.817 --> 01:13:22.237
It's too bad you came down here, Janet.

895
01:13:49.637 --> 01:13:52.757
I'm ready to get me Bradshaw 7425.

896
01:13:55.537 --> 01:13:58.297
Martin, this is...

897
01:14:01.117 --> 01:14:02.477
Martin!

898
01:14:04.377 --> 01:14:05.457
Operator.

899
01:14:12.317 --> 01:14:13.217
Chris!

900
01:14:13.297 --> 01:14:14.937
Chris, wake up!

901
01:14:16.917 --> 01:14:19.799
Can't explain. Quick,
we'll take the back stairs.

902
01:14:45.957 --> 01:14:48.137
You forgot, Paul.

903
01:14:48.517 --> 01:14:52.117
My profession is raising the dead.

904
01:15:22.257 --> 01:15:25.492
You're losing quite a bit
of blood, aren't you, Alexis?

905
01:15:30.037 --> 01:15:31.897
Spirits don't need any blood.

906
01:15:37.437 --> 01:15:41.417
Do you think bullets can harm us ghosts?

907
01:15:59.537 --> 01:16:02.557
Too bad you missed.

908
01:16:02.897 --> 01:16:08.517
Those were your last two shots.

909
01:16:08.597 --> 01:16:10.297
Oh,

910
01:16:10.737 --> 01:16:11.855
my God. Oh, my God.

911
01:16:13.937 --> 01:16:16.257
The police are here, my friend.

912
01:16:16.757 --> 01:16:18.637
Your gun is empty.

913
01:16:18.857 --> 01:16:24.277
But at least, you have been privileged
to witness my best performance.

914
01:16:24.357 --> 01:16:25.917
You made one little mistake.

915
01:16:25.997 --> 01:16:28.357
I started with seven shells.

916
01:16:28.477 --> 01:16:30.637
I have one in the chamber.

917
01:16:31.177 --> 01:16:32.706
I have one in the chamber.

918
01:16:41.597 --> 01:16:42.657
Alexis,

919
01:16:43.257 --> 01:16:44.937
you were wonderful.

920
01:16:45.017 --> 01:16:46.017
Yes.

921
01:16:46.257 --> 01:16:49.317
I guess there must be a
better side to my nature.

922
01:16:50.177 --> 01:16:52.177
But now you must forget me.

923
01:16:52.257 --> 01:16:53.317
No, never.

924
01:16:53.397 --> 01:16:56.097
I don't need a crystal to see
that your future is bright,

925
01:16:56.177 --> 01:16:57.177
Janet.

926
01:16:57.457 --> 01:16:59.497
Don't you go clinging to the past.

927
01:17:00.397 --> 01:17:04.877
I lived by feeding people's
desire to escape the present.

928
01:17:07.157 --> 01:17:10.717
But you can't escape for long.

929
01:17:11.277 --> 01:17:13.537
Alexis, I'm afraid for you.

930
01:17:14.277 --> 01:17:15.877
I'll be all right.

931
01:17:16.617 --> 01:17:20.517
I'll find some spirits
that want to contact people.

932
01:17:25.777 --> 01:17:27.937
Open the window, Janet, please.

933
01:17:35.457 --> 01:17:37.277
Fly away, my friend.

934
01:17:38.457 --> 01:17:40.677
I won't need you anymore.

